1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,840
Right now, you're listening to numbers.

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:05,240
Your phone got a long list of them, and

3
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:07,720
it's turning them back into my voice, more

4
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:10,660
than forty thousand numbers every second.

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,680
Stick around, because later

6
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:13,900
you'll hear what happens when

7
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:15,660
it doesn't get enough of them.

8
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:18,110
This is stop six.

9
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,110
The last stop in the desert.

10
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:22,130
Bob has already sent his friend

11
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:23,830
words across this canyon.

12
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:25,310
Last time, he even sent

13
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:26,610
a picture of his face.

14
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:28,350
And now his friend wants

15
00:00:28,370 --> 00:00:29,630
to hear his voice.

16
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:32,380
So Bob does the obvious thing.

17
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:33,320
He shouts.

18
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,120
But the canyon is wide,

19
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:36,960
and the wind is loud.

20
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:38,820
By the time his shout gets to the other

21
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:41,100
side, there's nothing left of it.

22
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:42,660
He's going to have to send his voice

23
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,160
the same way he sent everything else.

24
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:46,200
As numbers.

25
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:48,260
And for that, we first need to

26
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,680
know what sound actually is.

27
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:53,410
Sound is air shaking.

28
00:00:53,830 --> 00:00:55,630
When you talk, your throat pushes

29
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:58,050
the air back and forth, really fast.

30
00:00:58,070 --> 00:01:00,250
That air bumps into the air next

31
00:01:00,270 --> 00:01:02,130
to it, which bumps the air next to

32
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:04,770
that, all the way to someone's ear.

33
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:07,330
Their ear feels the air shaking, and

34
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:09,390
their brain hears it as your voice.

35
00:01:09,909 --> 00:01:11,749
A long time ago, people figured

36
00:01:11,769 --> 00:01:13,609
out how to draw that shaking.

37
00:01:13,889 --> 00:01:15,749
You talk into a big horn.

38
00:01:15,969 --> 00:01:18,189
At the other end, a tiny needle shakes

39
00:01:18,209 --> 00:01:20,149
along with your voice and scratches

40
00:01:20,169 --> 00:01:22,609
a line onto a moving strip of paper.

41
00:01:22,829 --> 00:01:25,029
What comes out is a wiggly line.

42
00:01:25,329 --> 00:01:27,769
A big wiggle means a loud sound.

43
00:01:27,949 --> 00:01:30,309
A fast wiggle means a high sound.

44
00:01:31,009 --> 00:01:33,009
Bob draws his voice the same way.

45
00:01:33,149 --> 00:01:36,089
But a wiggly line still isn't numbers.

46
00:01:36,349 --> 00:01:37,589
So he grabs a ruler.

47
00:01:37,609 --> 00:01:39,429
He measures how high the

48
00:01:39,449 --> 00:01:40,869
line is right here.

49
00:01:41,109 --> 00:01:43,709
Then a tiny bit later, he measures again.

50
00:01:43,929 --> 00:01:44,669
And again.

51
00:01:44,989 --> 00:01:46,069
And again.

52
00:01:46,389 --> 00:01:48,849
Every measurement is just one number.

53
00:01:49,049 --> 00:01:50,449
Put them in a row, and that

54
00:01:50,469 --> 00:01:52,369
row of numbers is his voice.

55
00:01:53,079 --> 00:01:54,559
He flashes the numbers across

56
00:01:54,579 --> 00:01:56,579
the canyon, one at a time.

57
00:01:56,759 --> 00:01:57,859
On the other side, his

58
00:01:57,879 --> 00:01:59,139
friend has a speaker.

59
00:01:59,419 --> 00:02:01,319
It's basically a paper circle that

60
00:02:01,339 --> 00:02:03,179
can move in and out, and each

61
00:02:03,199 --> 00:02:04,939
number tells it how far to push.

62
00:02:04,959 --> 00:02:08,299
Push, pull, push, pull,

63
00:02:08,319 --> 00:02:09,819
thousands of times.

64
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:11,799
The air starts shaking the same

65
00:02:11,819 --> 00:02:13,539
way Bob's throat shook it.

66
00:02:13,559 --> 00:02:15,539
And his friend hears Bob.

67
00:02:16,192 --> 00:02:17,272
There's a catch.

68
00:02:17,652 --> 00:02:19,072
How often do you measure?

69
00:02:19,432 --> 00:02:21,552
If Bob only measures now and then,

70
00:02:21,572 --> 00:02:23,512
he misses most of the wiggles.

71
00:02:23,692 --> 00:02:25,592
The speaker gets a rough, blocky

72
00:02:25,612 --> 00:02:27,632
copy, and it sounds awful.

73
00:02:28,092 --> 00:02:29,252
Here's the first line of this

74
00:02:29,272 --> 00:02:31,145
video again, measured only

75
00:02:31,165 --> 00:02:32,872
two thousand times a second.

76
00:02:33,352 --> 00:02:35,942
"Right now, you're listening to numbers." (the same line, measured 2,000 times a second)

77
00:02:36,692 --> 00:02:38,712
And here it is measured forty-four

78
00:02:38,732 --> 00:02:40,612
thousand times a second, which is what

79
00:02:40,632 --> 00:02:42,672
music on your phone usually uses.

80
00:02:43,102 --> 00:02:45,692
"Right now, you're listening to numbers." (the same line, measured 44,000 times a second)

81
00:02:46,442 --> 00:02:48,042
That's fast enough to catch every

82
00:02:48,062 --> 00:02:49,742
wiggle your ears can hear.

83
00:02:50,202 --> 00:02:52,702
Each measurement needs to be precise too.

84
00:02:53,002 --> 00:02:54,682
Eight lamps can only count to

85
00:02:54,702 --> 00:02:56,402
two hundred and fifty-five, so

86
00:02:56,422 --> 00:02:58,242
the line comes out a bit jagged.

87
00:02:58,542 --> 00:03:00,782
For music, computers use sixteen

88
00:03:00,802 --> 00:03:02,462
lamps for every measurement.

89
00:03:02,642 --> 00:03:04,022
That's enough to count past

90
00:03:04,175 --> 00:03:05,833
sixty-five thousand.

91
00:03:06,033 --> 00:03:09,093
So a three minute song is forty-four thousand

92
00:03:09,113 --> 00:03:11,533
numbers every second, with one list

93
00:03:11,553 --> 00:03:14,313
for each ear, for three whole minutes.

94
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:16,973
That's almost sixteen million numbers.

95
00:03:16,993 --> 00:03:18,913
For one song.

96
00:03:19,593 --> 00:03:20,973
The speaker in your phone does

97
00:03:20,993 --> 00:03:23,113
exactly what Bob's friend did.

98
00:03:23,153 --> 00:03:24,673
It reads the list and pushes

99
00:03:24,693 --> 00:03:26,793
the air, number by number.

100
00:03:26,813 --> 00:03:29,073
Which is how you're hearing me right now.

101
00:03:30,123 --> 00:03:32,023
Bob plants flag number six.

102
00:03:32,343 --> 00:03:33,943
That's every stop in the desert.

103
00:03:33,963 --> 00:03:35,623
When he got here, all

104
00:03:35,643 --> 00:03:37,523
he had was on and off.

105
00:03:37,823 --> 00:03:40,043
Now he can send words, pictures,

106
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:41,203
and even his voice.

107
00:03:41,743 --> 00:03:44,203
Next time, the fog finally lifts.
